justifications.evidence:
the two relations the fence names hold exactly, re-derived from the committed file with json.load: throttled_share 0.3690189573067196 == throttled_usec 5795334085321 / permitted_usec 15704705600000 (bit-exact float equality, absolute difference 0.0), and permitted_usec == nr_periods 39261764 x the 400000 quota == 15704705600000 (exact). The file alone, without the manifest, carries everything an interval share against a later reading on the same boot needs: boot_id and uptime_seconds establish the same boot and an order, cpu_max carries the quota, and all three period counters plus usage_usec are present, so a later reading yields delta(throttled_usec)/delta(permitted_usec) with delta(permitted_usec) = delta(nr_periods) x 400000. Demonstrated rather than argued: from a compute node I read the same slice over ssh twice, got the same boot_id 82c41dfa-a445-4fb2-8416-04b44c3d874c, and computed interval shares of 1.276 over the 2472.7 s from the committed reading (12624429662/9890800000) and 0.828 over the following 343.3 s (1136382398/1373200000). Corroboration of the verbatim claim from a source I did not produce: the run's own stream.jsonl line 1043 carries the ssh stdout of the committing read at 16:23:01Z, and its JSON block is byte-identical to the committed file (473 bytes, sha256 15f8e788c02369db; the file's od tail '6 
   } 
 } 
' matches the trailing bytes the worker dumped from the same stdout). The file's mtime 18:23 CEST equals 16:23Z. Caveat, already documented by the plan and therefore not a defect here: the ratio is unclamped and can exceed one, so the interval form is a draw rather than a level.
